---
export const prerender = true;

import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { datasetStats, fmt, pct } from '@/lib/stats';

// Same /healthz-derived numbers the homepage prints — never hand-edit these.
const stats = await datasetStats();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — rootfast"
  description="What the Rootfast native plant API is, how county-level nativity is derived, who maintains it, how often it refreshes, and what happens if it outgrows its hosting."
>
  <article class="max-w-content mx-auto px-6 lg:px-10">
    <header class="pt-12 pb-8">
      <p class="label flex items-baseline gap-3"><span>§</span><span>About the catalogue</span></p>
      <h1 class="mt-5 font-serif text-4xl sm:text-5xl text-ink leading-none tracking-tight">
        A small useful thing,
        <span class="block italic text-ink-2 mt-1">built carefully.</span>
      </h1>
    </header>

    <hr class="rule-strong" />

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-10 lg:gap-16 py-12">
      <section class="lg:col-span-7 prose-rf">
        <p class="font-serif text-xl italic text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          Rootfast answers a simple question — <em>I live here, what should I plant?</em> — and
          answers it with data, not opinion.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-serif text-2xl text-ink">Why this exists</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          The data already exists. USDA PLANTS knows which native species occur in every
          county. The Plant Hardiness Zone Map knows what survives the winter. The GBIF
          Backbone resolves the taxonomic mess across competing scientific authorities.
          Each is a beautiful piece of public infrastructure. None of them speak to each
          other in a useful way.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          Rootfast joins them, on a canonical taxonomic key, and serves the result as a
          plain HTTP API. The hard work isn't novel — it's stewardship. We've kept every
          source citation, every confidence rating, every &ldquo;USDA hasn't characterised
          this taxon yet.&rdquo; The data is honest about what it knows and what it doesn't.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-serif text-2xl text-ink">What it covers, today</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>The United States, nationwide.</strong> {fmt(stats.taxa)} plant taxa across
          {stats.states} of {stats.statesTotal} states and territories, {fmt(stats.counties)} counties,
          {fmt(stats.distributionRecords)} county-presence records with native/introduced status.
          USDA cultivation specs where USDA has formally characterised the species.
          iNaturalist photos for {pct(stats.photos, stats.taxa)} of taxa (CC-licensed, hot-linked
          from iNat, never rehosted). Every record carries a GBIF taxon key so additional
          sources join cleanly.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>USDA Plant Hardiness Zone Map (2023).</strong> {fmt(stats.hardinessZips)} US ZIP codes and
          {fmt(stats.hardinessCounties)} counties mapped to their canonical hardiness zone (1a–13b), joinable
          against each plant&apos;s minimum-temperature tolerance.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>ZIP codes.</strong> {fmt(stats.zipCounties)} ZIPs resolve to the county or counties
          they cover (US Census 2020 ZCTA crosswalk), so a single ZIP returns its counties,
          its hardiness zone, and the plants recorded there.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>How county nativity is derived.</strong> It isn&apos;t derived — it&apos;s
          USDA&apos;s own. USDA PLANTS publishes two different things: a coarse regional
          native status per plant (L48, Alaska, Hawaii, Canada, …) and, separately, a
          county distribution layer where every plant × county record carries its own
          <em>Native</em> or <em>Introduced</em> flag. Rootfast stores the county layer as-is.
          When USDA lists the same county under both flags we record <em>Both</em>; state
          and ZIP listings roll the county values up to one value per taxon. The regional
          status is kept on the plant record for reference but never used to infer a county.
        <p class="mt-4 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>Known gaps.</strong> Connecticut is mid-migration on the USDA side
          following its 2022 abolition of counties — USDA has the new planning regions
          in their gazetteer but no plant records linked yet. A handful of water-heavy
          coastal counties also fall outside their hardiness-zone polygon (centroid
          lands in water). PO-box-only ZIPs have no Census ZCTA and so no county.
          We&apos;re working in the open; the rough edges are real.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-serif text-2xl text-ink">Refresh cadence and versions</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          The loaded dataset is <strong class="mono">{stats.datasetVersion}</strong>, built from a USDA
          PLANTS pull on <span class="mono">{stats.usdaPulled}</span>. Dataset versions are
          date-based (<span class="mono">YYYY.MM</span>) and every refresh ships as a tagged
          <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="https://github.com/cjscrofani/rootfast/releases" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub Release ↗</a>
          with the full SQLite database and a Parquet set, so the data outlives the service.
          We re-pull USDA PLANTS and iNaturalist photos at least twice a year, and sooner when
          USDA publishes a notable change (Connecticut's county records, for instance).
          The live version and pull date are always in
          <a class="link-hover text-ink mono" href="https://api.rootfast.org/healthz">/healthz</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-serif text-2xl text-ink">Stability, deprecation, and what if it grows</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>/v1 is stable.</strong> Fields are only added, never renamed or removed.
          A breaking change ships as <span class="mono">/v2</span>, and <span class="mono">/v1</span>
          stays up for at least twelve months afterwards. Deprecations are announced in the
          <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="https://github.com/cjscrofani/rootfast/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CHANGELOG ↗</a>
          and signalled with a <span class="mono">Deprecation</span> response header.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>Hosting.</strong> The whole thing is built to fit Cloudflare's free tier,
          so it runs on a fixed daily database allowance; when that is spent, cached pages
          keep working and the rest waits for midnight UTC.
          If traffic outgrows it, the first lever is caching (responses are
          cached for a day) and the second is tighter anonymous rate limits with free
          keys for heavy users — never a paywall on the data, which is CC0 and downloadable.
          If the service ever has to go dark, the last release stays on GitHub.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-10 font-serif text-2xl text-ink">How it's built</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          A Python ETL fetches from USDA's undocumented public ArcGIS endpoints and
          REST API, one state at a time. A matcher calls GBIF's
          <code>/species/match</code> endpoint to assign each plant a canonical
          taxonomic key. iNaturalist photos are joined in by taxon id, with each
          photo's individual CC license preserved on the record. The merged dataset
          is written to Cloudflare D1 via a chunked-import script that works around
          a brittle upload path in modern wrangler; a small TypeScript Worker serves
          the API behind an OpenAPI 3.0 spec at
          <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="/docs">/docs</a>. The site you're
          reading is Astro on Cloudflare Pages.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
          Code is MIT-licensed, data is CC0. The whole thing is built to fit
          Cloudflare's free tier. Source — including ETL pipelines, schema migrations,
          and the discovery notes that explain how to find these undocumented USDA
          endpoints in the first place — is on GitHub:
          <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="https://github.com/cjscrofani/rootfast" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com/cjscrofani/rootfast ↗</a>.
        </p>

      </section>

      <aside class="lg:col-span-5 lg:pl-10 lg:border-l lg:border-rule space-y-10">
        <div>
          <p class="label">Status, in numbers</p>
          <dl class="mt-4 field-grid">
            <dt>Plant taxa</dt><dd class="mono">{fmt(stats.taxa)}</dd>
            <dt>States &amp; territories</dt><dd class="mono">{stats.states} / {stats.statesTotal}</dd>
            <dt>Counties</dt><dd class="mono">{fmt(stats.counties)}</dd>
            <dt>Distribution records</dt><dd class="mono">{fmt(stats.distributionRecords)}</dd>
            <dt>GBIF coverage</dt><dd class="mono text-native">100 %</dd>
            <dt>Photos (iNat)</dt><dd class="mono">{fmt(stats.photos)} · {pct(stats.photos, stats.taxa)}</dd>
            <dt>Hardiness ZIPs</dt><dd class="mono">{fmt(stats.hardinessZips)}</dd>
            <dt>ZIPs → counties</dt><dd class="mono">{fmt(stats.zipCounties)}</dd>
            <dt>Dataset</dt><dd class="mono">{stats.datasetVersion}</dd>
            <dt>USDA pull</dt><dd class="mono">{stats.usdaPulled}</dd>
          </dl>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="label">Sources</p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-ink-2">
            <li>
              <p class="text-ink font-medium">USDA PLANTS Database</p>
              <p class="mt-1">Taxonomy, distribution, native status, characteristics. Public domain.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p class="text-ink font-medium">USDA-ARS Plant Hardiness Zone Map (2023)</p>
              <p class="mt-1">ZIP and county hardiness zones. Public domain.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p class="text-ink font-medium">GBIF Backbone Taxonomy</p>
              <p class="mt-1">Canonical identity + synonym resolution. CC0.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p class="text-ink font-medium">iNaturalist</p>
              <p class="mt-1">Species photos, hot-linked under each photo&apos;s individual CC license.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="label">Who runs it</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
            Rootfast is built and maintained by
            <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="https://github.com/cjscrofani" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CJ Scrofani</a>,
            a developer in Montana, as a public-interest side project. There is no company
            behind it and no plan to charge for the data. The fastest way to reach the
            maintainer is a
            <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="https://github.com/cjscrofani/rootfast/issues" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub issue ↗</a>;
            the <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="/contact">contact form</a> works too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="label">Get involved</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-2 leading-relaxed">
            Issues, ideas, corrections, requests for specific regions — welcome.
            Rootfast is a quiet public-interest project. Bring your patch.
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm">
            <li>
              <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="https://github.com/cjscrofani/rootfast" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com/cjscrofani/rootfast ↗</a>
              <span class="text-ink-3"> — full source, schema, ETL scripts</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="https://github.com/cjscrofani/rootfast/issues" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open an issue ↗</a>
              <span class="text-ink-3"> — data fixes, feature requests, bug reports</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a class="link-hover text-ink" href="/docs">API documentation</a>
              <span class="text-ink-3"> — runnable examples, OpenAPI spec, rate limits</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
